00:05:54.060 --> 00:06:02.040
BERT score F1 of 0.8230. The N-gram overlap was relatively low, but semantic similarity was excellent.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.440
This work makes a compelling case for building privacy preserving domain-specific models,

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.960
even for languages that typically get ignored by the major labs.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.400
Moving over to the agent and tool use side, there's a genuinely practical new system called

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:22.120
model feature agent or MOFA. Instead of treating feature selection as a pure statistical problem,

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:27.960
MOFA uses an LLM's semantic understanding and quantitative feature information inside

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:33.280
structured prompts to perform sequential reasoning-based feature selection under real operational

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:38.520
constraints. In a true interest prediction task, it delivered accuracy gains while simultaneously

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:44.300
reducing feature complexity. It also surfaced high-order interactions that improved online engagement

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:50.380
metrics. For notification prediction, it selected compact feature subsets that boosted both accuracy

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:56.380
and inference speed. What I like about this work is that it shows LLM reasoning can add real value

00:06:56.380 --> 00:07:01.700
in production ML systems, where labeled data is scarce and you have strict latency and infrastructure

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:06.920
constraints. This feels like the kind of agent pattern that will quietly make its way into many

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:13.240
industrial ML pipelines over the next year. Another notable tool-oriented paper explores using

